      <section class="detail-block pd-section">
        <h2>What the ST101A Fixes</h2>
        <p class="pd-lede">If any of these is happening at your workstation, the cause is usually a surface charge nobody is measuring.</p>
        <ul class="fix-list">
          <li>
            <h3>Operators get shocks at the bench</h3>
            <p>Charge builds on the parts and the packaging and discharges through whoever handles them. A blower over the position neutralizes the area rather than one object.</p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <h3>Dust settles back onto parts you just cleaned</h3>
            <p>A charged surface pulls particles out of the air within seconds. Neutralize the bench and cleaning holds.</p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <h3>The bench blower is too big for the bench</h3>
            <p>At 90 × 170 × 260 mm and 3.3 kg this sits beside a microscope or a rework station without taking the working area.</p>
          </li>
        </ul>
      </section>

      <section class="detail-block pd-section">
        <h2>How It Works</h2>
        <p>Positive and negative ions are produced by corona discharge at the emitter points and carried to the work by the fan. Airflow is adjustable from 45 to 110 CFM: more air reaches further and neutralizes faster, less air is quieter and disturbs light parts less. Ion balance is held at 0 V ±10 V. The ST101A works on the alternating-current principle, and the high-voltage generator is built into the housing rather than supplied as a separate box.</p>
        <p>
          The process is self-limiting: once a surface reaches neutral it stops taking up ions. What decides
          whether it works in practice is whether the airflow actually reaches the charged surface &mdash; which
          is why coverage area, not ion output, is the figure to size a blower on. Verify the result with a
          static field meter rather than assuming it.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="detail-block pd-section">
        <h2>Specifications</h2>
        <div class="spec-table-wrap">
          <table class="spec-table">
            <tr><td>Model</td><td>ST101A</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Operating voltage</td><td>110 V / 60 Hz or 220 V / 50 Hz</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Power</td><td>7.5 W to 25 W</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Current consumption</td><td>0.25 A or 0.12 A</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Ion emission</td><td>Alternating current (AC)</td></tr>
            <tr><td>High-voltage generator</td><td>Built in</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Air volume</td><td>45–110 CFM, adjustable</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Air coverage</td><td>40 cm × 60 cm</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Ion balance</td><td>0 V ±10 V</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Operating temperature</td><td>−10 °C to 50 °C</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Dimensions</td><td>90 mm (L) × 170 mm (W) × 260 mm (H)</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Weight</td><td>3.3 kg</td></tr>
          </table>
        </div>
        <div class="pd-trust">
          <span>Built in our own Dongguan factory since 2013</span>
          <a href="/about">About Shidike &rarr;</a>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="detail-block pd-section">
        <h2>Decay Time &mdash; Measured</h2>
        <p>Measured decay times, taken directly in front of the unit. Decay slows with distance: at 300 mm the ST101A clears 1 kV in about 1.2 seconds, at 900 mm it takes around 4 seconds. Mount it as close to the charged work as the operator can tolerate.</p>
        <div class="spec-table-wrap">
          <table class="decay-table">
            <thead>
              <tr><th></th><th>300 mm</th><th>600 mm</th><th>900 mm</th></tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td>Positive decay</td><td>1.2 s</td><td>2.5 s</td><td>3.9 s</td></tr>
              <tr><td>Negative decay</td><td>1.3 s</td><td>2.6 s</td><td>4.1 s</td></tr>
              <tr><td>Offset voltage (+)</td><td>8 V</td><td>5 V</td><td>4 V</td></tr>
              <tr><td>Offset voltage (&minus;)</td><td>&minus;7 V</td><td>&minus;6 V</td><td>&minus;3 V</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
        <p class="decay-note">Charge decay measured from 1 kV to 100 V, unit running on 110/220 V, ambient 22 &deg;C and 75 %RH.</p>
        <p>Offset voltage is the residual charge the blower itself leaves behind. At 300 mm it stays within 8 V, and it falls further with distance — well inside the 0 V ±10 V balance figure quoted above.</p>
      </section>


      <div class="pd-mid-cta">
        <p>
          <strong>Not sure the ST101A is the right size?</strong>
          Send the area you need to cover, what the material is and the measured surface voltage &mdash;
          we will come back with a model and a mounting position.
        </p>
        <button class="quote-btn" type="button">Ask an Engineer</button>
      </div>

      <section class="detail-block pd-section">
        <h2>Where It Goes</h2>
        <p class="pd-lede">A blower neutralizes an area rather than a line. These are the positions the ST101A is sized for.</p>
        <div class="scenario-list">
          <div class="scenario-item">
            <h3>One operator position</h3>
            <p>An inspection bench, a rework station or a microscope position — the blower covers the working area in front of the operator.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="scenario-item">
            <h3>Electronics and SMT benches</h3>
            <p>Where components are handled outside their packaging and electrostatic discharge damages parts without leaving a mark.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="scenario-item">
            <h3>Packaging and labelling</h3>
            <p>Where charged film and labels cling, misfeed or attract dust at a manual station.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="detail-block pd-section">
        <h2>Bar or Blower, and Which Blower</h2>
        <p>
          Use an <a href="/products/static-eliminator">ionizing bar</a> when the material moves past a fixed
          point &mdash; off a roller, out of a tool, off a stack. A bar mounted at that point neutralizes faster
          and holds a tighter balance, and is cut to the exact width of the material. Use a blower when the
          charge sits on a fixed area, or when there is no way to mount a bar within its 1 m working range.
        </p>
        <p>
          Between the blowers it is a question of area:
          <a href="/products/static-eliminator/st-s200">ST-S200</a> 450 &times; 450 mm to build into a machine,
          <a href="/products/static-eliminator/st-101a">ST101A</a> 40 &times; 60 cm for one position,
          <a href="/products/static-eliminator/st-104a">ST104A</a> 60 &times; 120 cm for a wider bench, and
          <a href="/products/static-eliminator/st-1200">ST1200</a> 140 &times; 120 cm across a full line.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="detail-block faq-block pd-section">
        <h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
        <div class="faq-list">
          <details class="faq-item">
            <summary><h3 class="faq-question">What area does the ST101A cover?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-answer">40 × 60 cm. That is a coverage area in front of the unit, not a working distance — it is sized for one operator position rather than a line.</p>
          </details>
          <details class="faq-item">
            <summary><h3 class="faq-question">How much airflow does it deliver?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-answer">45 to 110 CFM, adjustable. Turn it up for faster neutralization, down for quieter running and less disturbance to light parts.</p>
          </details>
          <details class="faq-item">
            <summary><h3 class="faq-question">What ion balance does it hold?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-answer">0 V ±10 V.</p>
          </details>
          <details class="faq-item">
            <summary><h3 class="faq-question">Will it fit on a working bench?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-answer">It is 90 × 170 × 260 mm and 3.3 kg, so it stands beside a microscope or a rework station without taking the working area.</p>
          </details>
          <details class="faq-item">
            <summary><h3 class="faq-question">What power supply does it need?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-answer">110 V / 60 Hz or 220 V / 50 Hz, drawing 0.25 A or 0.12 A respectively.</p>
          </details>
          <details class="faq-item">
            <summary><h3 class="faq-question">Should I use a blower or a bar?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-answer">A bar neutralizes material moving past it on a line. A blower carries ionized air to a fixed area. If the material moves, choose a bar; if the charge sits still at a bench, choose a blower.</p>
          </details>
          <details class="faq-item">
            <summary><h3 class="faq-question">ST101A, ST104A or ST1200?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-answer">The ST101A covers 40 × 60 cm for one position, the ST104A 60 × 120 cm for a wider bench or two operators, and the ST1200 140 × 120 cm across a full line.</p>
          </details>
                  <details class="faq-item">
            <summary><h3 class="faq-question">How fast does the ST101A neutralize?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-answer">From 1 kV down to 100 V it takes about 1.2 seconds at 300 mm, 2.5 seconds at 600 mm and 3.9 seconds at 900 mm, tested at 22 °C and 75 %RH. Residual offset voltage stays within 8 V positive and −7 V negative across that range.</p>
          </details>
</div>
        <div class="pd-mid-cta" style="margin-top:30px;">
